## @dnd-kit Drag and Drop

### Package Setup (New API)

Use the new `@dnd-kit/react` and `@dnd-kit/helpers` packages. The legacy `@dnd-kit/core` + `@dnd-kit/sortable` packages are replaced.

```json
{
  "@dnd-kit/react": "^1.x",
  "@dnd-kit/helpers": "^1.x",
  "@dnd-kit/collision": "^1.x"
}
```

### Provider

Use `DragDropProvider` — it replaces the legacy `DndContext`. No `SortableContext` is needed.

```tsx
import { DragDropProvider } from '@dnd-kit/react';

function App() {
  return (
    <DragDropProvider
      onDragEnd={(event) => { /* handle drop */ }}
    >
      {children}
    </DragDropProvider>
  );
}
```

### Sortable Lists

Use `useSortable` with `id` and `index`. The `OptimisticSortingPlugin` handles visual reordering during drag automatically.

```tsx
import { useSortable, isSortable } from '@dnd-kit/react/sortable';
import { directionBiased } from '@dnd-kit/collision';
import { memo } from 'react';

const SortableItem = memo(function SortableItem({ id, index }: { id: string; index: number }) {
  const { ref, isDragging } = useSortable({
    id,
    index,
    collisionDetector: directionBiased,
  });

  return (
    <div ref={ref} style={{ opacity: isDragging ? 0.5 : 1 }}>
      {id}
    </div>
  );
});
```

### State Management on Drag End

Use `isSortable` type guard. Compare `initialIndex`/`index` and `initialGroup`/`group`.

```tsx
<DragDropProvider
  onDragEnd={(event) => {
    if (event.canceled) return;
    const { source } = event.operation;
    if (isSortable(source)) {
      setItems((items) => move(items, event));
    }
  }}
>
```

Or manual state management for full control:

```tsx
onDragEnd={(event) => {
  if (event.canceled) return;
  const { source } = event.operation;
  if (isSortable(source)) {
    const { initialIndex, index, initialGroup, group } = source;
    if (initialGroup === group) {
      // Reorder within same list
    } else {
      // Cross-list transfer
    }
  }
}}
```

### Multi-List / Kanban

Use `group`, `type`, and `accept` to control which items can be sorted where:

```tsx
const { ref } = useSortable({
  id: item.id,
  index,
  group: columnId,
  type: 'card',
  accept: 'card',
});
```

### Drag Overlay

- Never render a component that uses `useSortable` or `useDraggable` inside the drag overlay — causes ID collisions
- Create a separate presentational component for the overlay
- Use ref forwarding or wrapper node pattern

### Accessibility — Non-Negotiable

- Always register BOTH `PointerSensor` and `KeyboardSensor`
- Draggable activator elements MUST be focusable (`<button>`, `<a>`, or `tabIndex={0}`)
- Provide `aria-label` or `aria-describedby` on draggable handles
- Use `sortableKeyboardCoordinates` for sortable keyboard navigation
- Respect reduced motion (handled automatically by the Feedback plugin)
- Custom screen reader announcements via `announcements` prop

### Sensors

```tsx
import { PointerSensor, KeyboardSensor } from '@dnd-kit/react';

<DragDropProvider
  sensors={(defaults) => [
    ...defaults,
    PointerSensor.configure({ activationConstraint: { distance: 8 } }),
    KeyboardSensor.configure({ offset: 25 }),
  ]}
>
```

- Touch: use 250–400ms activation delay to distinguish from scrolling
- Pointer: use `distance: 8` to prevent accidental drags on click

### Collision Detection

- `directionBiased` — optimized for sortable lists (recommended default)
- `closestCenter` — forgiving, good for general sortable lists
- `closestCorners` — good for grid layouts
- `rectIntersection` — precise, default for non-sortable
- `pointerWithin` — high-precision, pointer must be inside droppable
- Compose algorithms for mixed interfaces (e.g., sortable list + trash zone)

### Animation & Performance

- `useSortable` returns `isDragging` and `isDropping` for styling transitions
- Wrap sortable items in `memo()` to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- For large lists (100+ items), consider virtualization
- Disable transitions for items that are not actively involved in the drag

### Integration with External State (React Query, Zustand)

When using sortable lists with data fetching:
- Maintain a local `items` state; render from it, not directly from query data
- Only sync fetched data into local state when no drag is in progress
- Snapshot state on `onDragStart`, revert on cancel

### dnd-kit Anti-Patterns

| Never | Always |
|---|---|
| Use `useSortable` inside drag overlay | Render a presentational component |
| Duplicate IDs across one `DragDropProvider` | Unique IDs for all items |
| Skip `KeyboardSensor` | Register both Pointer + Keyboard sensors |
| Non-focusable drag handles (`<div>`) | `<button>` or `tabIndex={0}` |
| Use legacy `DndContext` / `SortableContext` | `DragDropProvider` + `useSortable` |
| Compare `source.id === target.id` with optimistic sorting | Use `initialIndex` / `index` / `group` |

